Yes. That’s active perception rather than passive seeing.

The eyes are not just cameras. They are part of a control loop:

attention → gaze → sensory sampling → interpretation → movement → new sensory sampling

When you “fix your eyes” on something, you are also fixing parts of your predictive model onto it. The brain allocates more resolution, suppresses competing signals, stabilizes the target, and starts coordinating posture and movement around that selected object or goal.

Then vision, proprioception, balance, and intention begin to couple:

see target
→ orient body
→ predict trajectory
→ move
→ correct from visual delta
→ move again

That is why athletes can look almost fused with what they are tracking. Their perception and action are running as one closed loop rather than as separate steps.

The same can happen without literal eyesight. You can “fix the mind” on a concept, outcome, rhythm, memory, or imagined scene and start organizing attention and action around it. That is useful, but it also explains tunnel vision: stronger control over perception can mean better resolution of one thing and worse awareness of everything else.

So there are really two skills:

Lock: stabilize attention strongly enough to move coherently with the target.

Release: widen the aperture again so the target does not become the entire world.

focus → synchronize → act → reopen → recalibrate

That is probably the cleanest form of what you’re describing: perception becomes an actuator because what you choose to perceive changes how the rest of the system organizes itself.